WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.752
Whereas IPv4 addresses are 32 bits in length, IPv6 addresses are 128 bits, or, technically, 128 binary 1s and 0s in length.

00:10.752 --> 00:23.720
IPv6 addressing became necessary as the IPv4 address pool started to become scarce. To put this in perspective, the total number of IPv4 addresses is about four billion.

00:23.720 --> 00:39.808
The total number of IPv6 addresses? 340 undecillion, or 3.4 x 10 to the 38th power. That is a lot. Here is an example of an IPv6 address.

00:39.808 --> 00:53.514
Though they are 128 bits in length, they are represented as hexadecimal numbers, 32 of them to be exact. Subnetting in IPv6 is quite a bit different than that of IPv4.

00:53.514 --> 01:03.190
First, IPv6 networks have /64 prefixes, meaning the first 64 bits in an IPv6 address define the network.

01:03.190 --> 01:14.674
Within that prefix, the network address is the first 48 bits of the prefix or the first three groups of numbers. The fourth group of numbers represents the subnet address.

01:14.674 --> 01:21.538
The last four groups of numbers, the last 64 bits, represent the device address.

